<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Okomfo Anokye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1700</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Okomfo Anokye was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> priest who, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> around 1700. Tradition holds that he conjured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a> from the heavens, which became the sacred symbol of Asante unity. He also promulgated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventy-seven-laws">Seventy-Seven Laws</a> and helped lead the victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a> at Feyiase in 1701.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to Akuapem tradition, Okomfo Anokye was born around 1655 at Awukugua, in the Nifa Division of the Okere state, the son of Ano and Yaa Anubea; his original name is given as Kwame Anokye Frimpon Kotobre. A tradition holds that he was born holding a short white cow's tail in one hand and something clenched in the other, which he opened before his father with the words Ano kye, Ano see, in the Guan language. From this, it is said, he took the name Anokye.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> succeeded to the throne of the Kumaseman state, at some point between about 1680 and 1695, Anokye was his adviser and chief priest. The two carried on the expansion of their predecessors among the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> forest states around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, defeating the Akan Domaa to the north-west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a> to the south. Using both the political influence of his priesthood and the spiritual ties it created, Anokye helped turn their loose alliance into a national union; Kumasi was made the capital, a state council of chiefs was formed, competing traditions of origin were suppressed and the army was reorganised.</p><p>The war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a>, fought from 1699 to 1701, went badly at first, but tradition holds that when the Denkyira army reached the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, Anokye's incantations caused defections among its generals. The victory broke Denkyira's hegemony and gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> deed of rent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina Castle</a>, opening the coast to their traders and drawing them into the commerce and politics of the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. A kola tree at Feyiase, said to have grown from a seed Anokye spat, was cut down in 2023.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Anokye's death is told in several ways. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> died in 1717, he is said to have gone to fetch the key to death so that no one would die again, ordering that no one should cry while he was away; when the women cried, he never returned. Another tradition says he went into a trance and never woke. Later research suggests he suffered from asthma, which may have caused his death in his sleep, dated to between 1717 and 1719. A sword he is believed to have planted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> stands at the Komfo Anokye Teaching Hospital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Okomfo Anokye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He recognised that while they were divided the Asante could not challenge Denkyira, and he allied himself with the priest <b>Okomfo Anokye</b>, whose summoning of the Golden Stool from the heavens became the most powerful founding tradition in Asante history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akwamu">Akwamu</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akwamu">Akwamu</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
